  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/e/e9/Tales_to_Astonish_Vol_1_53.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "While demonstrating for an orphanage, a concealed Porcupine shoots Giant-Man, causing him to fall and fracture his ankle.&nbsp; Porcupine then decides to join the local chapter of the Giant-Man and Wasp fan club, planning to accompany its members when they visit Giant-Man, who is now laid up with the hurt ankle.&nbsp; They'll all wear costumes of his old villains, except Porcupine's will be the real thing!\n\nThe unsuspecting Giant-Man and Wasp usher the fan club right in.&nbsp; Porcupine sends the Wasp out to his car to pick up a \"gift,\" and the moment she leaves, he floods the room with sleeping gas.&nbsp; Hank is hampered by the ankle, but he towers above the gas and has time to attack his foe while the kids fall victim to the gas.&nbsp; And though it successfully drives the Porcupine away, the villain informs him as he leaves that his car is booby-trapped, and that the Wasp is his prisoner.&nbsp; Giant-Man needs to leave him alone if he ever wants to see the Wasp alive again!\n\nGiant-Man hobbles down to the car, imagining the sequence of events that led to Jan being trapped, but he's not in time to stop the car from zooming off!&nbsp; Porcupine locks Jan up, demanding that she tell him Giant-Man's true identity.&nbsp; Hank tries to find the villain with ants, but Porcupine has equipped his stuff with DDT, which prevents the ants from reporting back.&nbsp; We learn that Porcupine actually <i>wants</i> Jan to escape so that he can follow her back to their secret headquarters.&nbsp; She does, and Porcupine tracks her all the way to their New Jersey home.\n\nWhen Jan finally arrives, Hank opens up about how he didn't realize how much he needed her.&nbsp; But Porcupine has <i>done</i> it -- he's tracked her back to their HQ!&nbsp; Soon he crashes in, stops the Wasp with fly paper, and hits Giant-Man with sleeping gas while he is still weakened by the ankle injury.&nbsp; As they tussle, Porcupine tries to grab at the pills in Giant-Man's belt and is able to get some, which he pops in his mouth, but -- <b><i>horrors</i></b> -- he's taken the wrong ones!&nbsp; He begins shrinking, and because he swallowed more than one, he <i>keeps</i> shrinking -- apparently to microbe size -- and ultimately vanishes!\n\nSince the Porcupine has conveniently disposed of <i>himself</i>, Giant-Man frees the Wasp from the fly paper, and the two share a happy moment together.",
  "grade_one_liner": "The Porcupine hides amidst layers of fan-club nonsense.&nbsp; You keep reading for Hank and Jan, even when the comic gives you every reason not to.",
  "subtitle": "Revenge by Way of the White Pages",
  "review_text": "[ASSET: left | 360pounds | width=17%] [ASSET: right | FanClub | width=10%] The Porcupine returns (see [LINK:1963:TTA_48_ant0|TTA #48|2026-11-22] for his first appearance) and immediately causes Giant-Man to fracture an ankle (and we as readers at <i>least</i> learn that Hank's giant form weighs 360 pounds).\n[BR]\n[BR] The attack itself wasn't as abhorrent, but when Porcupine looks up the local chapter of the Giant-Man and Wasp fan club (apparently in the white pages, as shown on page 4), he convinces the kids to pay a visit to the injured Giant-Man.\n\nWhile it was nice that page 5 informed us that Giant-Man rents a \"full-floor loft... as a combination gym and lab\" (which <i>alone</i> is an oddity -- who combines a gymnasium with a <i>laboratory</i>?), the <i>truly </i>bewildering revelation was that [SPAN: severity=info; text=the fan club knew exactly where he was recuperating.]&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=warning; fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold-italic; text=What?]\n\nAnd this isn't even to <i>mention</i> the fan club's existence in the <i>first </i>place, <i>especially </i>one that is actually listed in the <b>white pages</b>!&nbsp; Don't forget that it was only four issues ago that Ant-Man even became Giant-Man, and there's already a \"Giant-Man and Wasp Fan Club\" listing to be found in the white pages?&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=warning; fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold-italic; text=Seriously?]\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(It's true that last month's issue burned through \"months\" of story time, but even so, that's a <i>remarkably</i> fast turnaround time!)]\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=larger; text=The story wasn't even five pages old, and I was already losing interest!]\n\nThe ease with which the Porcupine infiltrated the fan club and got within striking distance of his chosen prey was gag-worthy.&nbsp; Not only <i>that</i>, but when he \"just remembered\" there was a \"gift\" for Giant-Man out in his [SPAN: severity=warning; fonttype=bold; text=car] and asked Wasp to go get it for him, shouldn't the fact that he was old enough to drive have been enough of a warning for the heroes?&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(The other kids were <i>clearly</i> not of driving age.)]\n\n[SPAN: severity=warning; fontsize=larger; fonttype=italic; text=Of course not!]\n[BR] Logic would have spoiled the plot, and we <b>can't have <i>that</i> now</b>, can we?&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=error; fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold-italic; text=Ugh!]\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[ASSET: left | OverwroughtFury | width=12%] The Wasp's capture aside, I thought Hank's fury as seen on page 11 was a bit overwrought.&nbsp; I can <i>understand</i> that Hank was angry, but by allowing the fan club to march its way right into his gym/lab, [SPAN: severity=warning; text=he has only himself to blame!]\n\nThe story finally shows a pulse on page 12, where we learn of Porcupine's plan to <i>allow</i> the Wasp to escape so that she would lead him straight to \"Giant-Man's home\" in New Jersey.\n[ASSET: center | TrackingWasp | width=67%]\n\n[SPAN: severity=success; text=It was a sensible enough plan], but it begs the question:\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=info; text=Why would the Wasp head to the New Jersey home and <i>not</i> back to the downtown gym/lab?]\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(But as I mentioned above, logic abandoned this story long ago...)]\n\n[ASSET: left | HankNeedsJan | width=12%] The second (and actually <i>better</i> moment) was on page 13, where Hank <i>finally</i> realizes exactly what Jan means to him.&nbsp; As you can see on the panel to the left, even <i>Jan</i> is so taken aback by his stunning admission that she demands a moment to return to normal size to allow him the chance to \"put your arms around me while you say that!\"&nbsp; It was an<i> </i>[SPAN: severity=success; text=excellent character beat] hidden amidst the over-dramatization and nonsense.\n\nAnd... if I may say it... it plays right along with my comments made during my [LINK:1963:TTA_48_ant0|TTA #48|2026-11-22] review.\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=Just like last time, the Porcupine's presence is simply window dressing around moments of true character development.]\n\nBut of course, the story still had pages to fill, and there needed to be a resolution...\n\n[DIVIDER]\nAfter four pages of uninspired action, Porcupine manages to snatch at Giant-Man's belt and steal some of his size-changing capsules, popping them all into his mouth, intending to grow to such a size as to <i>dwarf </i>even Giant-Man!\n\nBut in his haste, he naturally grabs the [SPAN: severity=error; fonttype=italic; text=wrong ones], and just like that, he shrinks to microbe size and out of sight.\n[ASSET: center | ShrinkingAway | width=67%]\n\nThe [SPAN: severity=success; fonttype=bold; text=day is saved]\n\nI suppose it won't be long before Porcupine finds himself in the same \"micro-world\" that Dr. Doom discovered after pulling much the same maneuver back in last year's [LINK:1962:FF_10|FF #10|2026-05-01] -- a world we got to see firsthand in this year's [LINK:1963:FF_16|FF #16|2026-09-14], which incidentally guest-starred Ant-Man himself.\n\nYou have to wonder if he'll even run into Princess Pearla down there...\n[BR] But again... that would require actual <b>logic</b>, and... [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=<i>well</i>... I've already gone <i>over</i> that...]\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nI'm largely speechless.&nbsp; I nicknamed this title [SPAN: fontsize=larger; fonttype=italic; text=Tales to Abolish], and I stick by it, because for the most part, it remains the most gag-inducing book that Marvel publishes (at least since [SPAN: fontsize=larger; fonttype=italic; text=Hulk] was discontinued).\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; text=The Porcupine was lame the <i>first</i> time he appeared, and this did nothing to make readers yearn for his return!]\n\nIn fact, I hope to <i>never</i> see this Porcupine character again -- [SPAN: severity=error; fonttype=bold; text=he's an idiot.]\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(And by bringing him back, Stan isn't doing himself any favors in that department <i>either</i>!)]\n\nThe plot was mostly boring, unimaginative, and <i>way</i> overplayed.&nbsp; Furthermore, we are left to assume that Giant-Man's fractured ankle was healed by the time the issue is over.&nbsp; I'll be <i>shocked</i> if next issue even <i>mentions</i> the injury.\n\n[SPAN: severity=warning; fontsize=larger; text=The real villain in this story was a complete <b>lack of logic</b>, <i>and it lost every fight</i>.]\n\nEven so, [SPAN: severity=success; text=there were still good moments to be found.]\n\nThe story does at least remind us that Giant-Man's identity is [SPAN: severity=info; text=still a secret] (as is that of the Wasp).&nbsp; Since previous issues depicted Hank standing around talking with the police while he was Ant-Man, it was easy to forget this.&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=info; text=This story at least drives that point home better than most.]\n\n[SPAN: severity=success; fontsize=larger; text=Even better was seeing Hank <i>finally</i> confess his feelings toward Jan on page 13.]\n\nBut aside from these moments, the issue delivered little to justify its cover price.\n[BR] And that fan club...&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=warning; fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold-italic; text=Come on, man!]\n\nLet's face it folks:\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=info; text=If you're missing this issue in your collection, it's hard to justify spending actual money on it!]",
  "side_note": [
    "<b>The Wonderful Wasp Tells a Tale:</b>  \"When Wakes the Colossus!\"\n\nBefore Jan leaves for the orphans, she gives Hank a preview of the tale she's about to spin, which is about a warlord named Mingo on a far-off planet, intent to prey on more primitive races on their world.&nbsp; Their conquest of Asikii race goes awry when a stone colossus erected to keep the Asikii in constant fear instead mysteriously awakens and attacks the conquerors, all thanks to the fact that, though \"<i>mortals</i> may be evil, but the <i>supernatural powers are not</i>!\"\n\nJan gets mad at Hank when she finds he wasn't paying attention, making him wonder \"...what makes that gal so <i>excitable</i>?\"\n\nIt's only another \"tale to astonish\" dressed up to shove the Wasp to the forefront."
  ],
  "highlight": "Porcupine weaponizes a Giant-Man fan club, kidnaps the Wasp, and accidentally initiates an unexpected breakthrough for Hank and Jan."
}